.TH ROAST 1 "2026" "roast" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
roast \- render Markdown for reading
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B roast
.RB [ \-u ]
.RB [ \-c | \-p ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR N ]
.RI [ FILE ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B roast
turns Markdown source into a terminal document. Where
.BR salt (1)
never changes a byte, roast exists to change them: headings lose
their #s and gain bold green (the top two levels underlined), task
items become \[u2610] and \[u2611], bullets become \[u2022] (\[u25E6] and \[u25AA] as they
nest), emphasis markers vanish into real bold and italics, inline
code drops its backticks, blockquotes grow a \[u2503] bar, horizontal
rules span the terminal, tables align into ruled columns, and
fenced code blocks lose their fences but keep syntax highlighting
in the fence's language \- the same engine salt uses.
.PP
Links become OSC 8 terminal hyperlinks (clickable where supported);
.B \-u
prints the url after the link text instead. Input is processed a
line at a time \- only tables buffer, since they need their widths \-
so
.B tail \-f NOTES.md | roast
stays live.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-u
Print urls after link text.
.TP
.B \-c
Color even when piped (for
.BR "less \-R" ).
.TP
.B \-p
No color. Glyph transforms still apply \- that is the point.
.TP
.BI \-w " N"
Width for horizontal rules (default: the terminal's).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 for unreadable or binary files, 2 for usage errors.
.SH BUGS
Table column alignment markers (:---:) are parsed but everything is
left-aligned. Double-width characters drift table padding slightly.
Setext (underlined) headings are not recognized.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR salt (1),
.BR psh (1)
.SH LORE
You salt a pistachio to keep it what it is; you roast it to turn it
into something better. Part of the psh armory. \[u1F331]
